-> **✏️ Example (illustrative — replace).** Escala tells a volunteer fire corps whether the people on today's *piquete* can actually crew the vehicles, given today's fire risk. It is an Angular PWA backed by two independently deployable components (ADR 0002). `escala-api` (Spring Boot) owns what the piquete needs: people, rosters, slots, unavailability, requests and audit. `elegibilidade-worker` (Spring Boot, Java) works out who can fill it: it runs the deterministic eligibility rules (ADR 0007), polls IPMA fire risk hourly (ADR 0010) and runs the AI extraction, but the AI never decides eligibility. The two exchange events through RabbitMQ with a transactional outbox, idempotency keys, retries and a dead-letter queue (ADR 0005, ADR 0009), and each owns its own PostgreSQL database (ADR 0003). Everything runs in Docker Compose, is built and deployed by GitHub Actions, and is observed with Prometheus and Grafana. Escala is about readiness, never dispatch.
+Escala is a mobile roster for one volunteer fire corps: members see their shifts, say when they cannot serve, swap with a colleague, confirm they are coming, and receive cover requests only if they are free and qualified (**D-00**, UJ1 / UJ3 / UJ6). It is an Angular PWA with two independently deployable Spring Boot services ([ADR 0002](../adr/0002-split-backend-into-two-independently-deployable-components.md)). `escala-api` owns people, the published schedule, unavailability, swaps, cover requests and audit. `elegibilidade-worker` ([ADR 0015](../adr/0015-implement-elegibilidade-worker-in-java-with-spring-boot.md)) runs deterministic function and eligibility checks ([ADR 0007](../adr/0007-deterministic-eligibility-engine-ai-never-decides.md)), extracts unavailability from free text, and polls IPMA so today's fire risk can be *shown* ([ADR 0010](../adr/0010-poll-ipma-hourly-and-cache-the-last-valid-value.md)). AI never decides who is eligible, who may swap, or who is asked to cover; explanations of failed checks are template text per rule code (RF22, RF34 Won't). The services exchange events through RabbitMQ with a transactional outbox, idempotency keys, retries and a dead-letter queue ([ADR 0005](../adr/0005-use-rabbitmq-as-message-broker.md), [ADR 0009](../adr/0009-transactional-outbox-and-idempotent-consumers.md)). Each owns its own PostgreSQL database ([ADR 0003](../adr/0003-one-postgresql-database-per-component.md)). The stack runs in Docker Compose, is built by GitHub Actions, and is observed with Prometheus and Grafana. Escala is about readiness, never dispatch.
 
